<p><strong>Finding the shortest path</strong><br>
When tasked with a graph problem that involves finding the shortest path be=
tween two nodes, BFS should come to mind. From the start node, BFS works by=
 exploring all neighbors at a distance of 1 from the start node, followed b=
y exploring nodes at a distance of 2, and so on. This means that once we fi=
nd the end node, we=E2=80=99ve reached it via the shortest possible distanc=
e.</p>
<p>So, let=E2=80=99s use <strong>level-order traversal</strong>, a variant =
of BFS, to find the shortest path in this graph, where each level we traver=
se represents nodes that are a specific distance from the start node:</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
;"><img alt=3D"Image represents a three-stage visualization of a breadth-fi=
