<p>As we can see, we managed to find the end node via the shortest path. To=
 return the length of this path, we return dist, plus 1, to include the sta=
rt node. If you=E2=80=99re unfamiliar with how level-order traversal works,=
 review the <em>Matrix Infection</em> problem in this chapter.</p>
<p>Note that during traversal, we need to ensure we don=E2=80=99t revisit p=
reviously traversed strings. By storing the visited strings in a <strong>ha=
sh set</strong>, we can quickly check if a string was already visited.</p>
<p>Now, let=E2=80=99s figure out how to build the above graph.</p>
<p><strong>Building the graph</strong><br>
We can represent the graph as an adjacency list, where each word has a list=
 of all of its neighboring words:</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
;"><img alt=3D"Image represents a graph data structure depicted in a textua=
l format.  The text is labeled 'graph:' and presents a series of key-value =
