n-sequence/image-13-06-7-HMFW2QE6.svg" style=3D"color: transparent;"></div>=
</figure>
<p>The challenge here is finding each word=E2=80=99s neighbors. Consider th=
e word =E2=80=9Cred=E2=80=9D. One way we can identify all words that are on=
e letter different from =E2=80=9Cred=E2=80=9D is to generate all possible w=
ords by changing each letter in =E2=80=9Cred=E2=80=9D to every other letter=
 in the alphabet. For each of these generated words, we check if it exists =
in the dictionary. If it does, it=E2=80=99s a neighbor of =E2=80=9Cred=E2=
=80=9D:</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
;"><img alt=3D"Image represents a tree-like data structure, possibly illust=
rating a coding pattern related to string manipulation or data partitioning=
.  The root node is labeled 'red'.  Three branches emanate from the root, l=
abeled '_ed', 'r_d', and 're_'. Each branch further subdivides into a serie=
